@page "/undo-long-lists"
@implements IDisposable

<PageTitle>Undo, redo &amp; long lists · Borm</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Undo, redo &amp; long lists</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        A stock list of five hundred lines, shown twenty at a time, with Undo and Redo beside the Save button. Paged
        rather than virtualised, so every line stays reachable: the error summary, focus-the-first-error and the pager's
        own "show the first" turn to the page a problem is on.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-note">
    Change a few quantities, remove a line, move one - then press Undo, again and again: each answer comes back with
    what it set off, a removed line as itself. Clear a line's product on page 7, go back to page 1 and press Save: the summary
    lists it, and its link turns to that page and puts the caret in the box. Two changes to the same field within a
    second are one undo.
</div>

<div class="bb-demo">
    <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">Live form</span></div>
    <BormView State="_state" ShowUndo="true" ShowErrorSummary="true" OnValidSubmit="HandleSubmit" SubmitText="Save stock" />
</div>

@if (_submitted)
{
    <div class="bb-note bb-note-success demo-result"><strong>Saved ✓</strong> @_model.Lines.Count lines, @_model.Total.ToString("N0") items in all.</div>
}

<details class="bb-source demo-source">
    <summary class="btn btn-secondary">View source</summary>
    <pre class="bb-code"><code>@Source</code></pre>
</details>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> What one undo takes back</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Everything one answer set off: the value, a field a condition then hid and cleared, a sibling a change handler
            filled in. They come back together, and the handler is not run again. Computed values are not recorded; they follow.</li>
        <li>Adding, removing, moving, copying and clearing rows are changes like any other, and undoing a removal puts back the
            same row - with whatever it held - rather than a copy.</li>
        <li>Two writes to one field within a second are one change, so a field that writes as the user types is not undone a
            character at a time; the tidying a field does as it is left (<code>Trim()</code>, a mask) belongs to that answer.</li>
        <li>A value put back is judged again, so an error the user was shown comes back with the value that caused it.
            <code>Reset()</code> and restoring a draft start the history again.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The buttons are named for the field they act on ("Undo the change to Product"), keep the focus so they can be pressed
        again, and announce what they did. Ctrl+Z inside a text box still undoes the typing in that box, as the browser always
        has. For buttons of your own: <code>state.UndoAsync()</code>, <code>RedoAsync()</code>, <code>CanUndo</code>,
        <code>CanRedo</code> and <code>NextUndoPath</code>; <code>HistoryLimit</code> is how many changes are kept - none by
        default, 100 once <code>ShowUndo</code> is on.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Paged, not virtualised</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>PageSize(20)</code> shows twenty rows at a time, with Previous and Next, a page picker and the rows on screen
        ("21–40 of 500"), and says how many rows on other pages need attention, with a button to the first of them. Every way of
        reaching a row still reaches it: the error summary's links and focus-the-first-error turn to the row's page first, and
        adding, copying or moving a row turns to the page it lands on. A virtualised list would leave the rows out of view out
        of the page, where neither the keyboard, the error summary nor a screen reader could reach them; a page is always
        whole. <code>x-pageSize</code> carries it through JSON Schema.
    </p>
</div>

@code {
    private const string Source =
"""
@page "/undo-long-lists"

<BormView State="_state" ShowUndo="true" ShowErrorSummary="true" OnValidSubmit="HandleSubmit" SubmitText="Save stock" />

@code {
    private readonly Invoice _model = new();
    private BormState _state = default!;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        for (var i = 1; i <= 500; i++)
            _model.Lines.Add(new InvoiceLine { Description = $"Item {i:000}", Quantity = 1 + i % 9, UnitPrice = 1 });

        var schema = BormBuilder.For<Invoice>()
            .Title("Stock")
            .Array(x => x.Lines, line => line
                    .Field(l => l.Description, f => f.Label("Product").Required())
                    .Field(l => l.Quantity, f => f.Range(0, 9999)),
                f => f.PageSize(20).Attr("itemNoun", "line"))
            .Computed(x => x.Total, m => m.Lines.Sum(l => (decimal)l.Quantity), dependsOn: [nameof(Invoice.Lines)],
                f => f.Label("Items in stock"))   // the model is an invoice; this list is a stock
            .Build();

        _state = new BormState(schema, new BormModelDataAccessor(_model));
    }

    public void Dispose() => _state.Dispose();
}
""";

    private readonly Invoice _model = new();
    private BormState _state = default!;
    private bool _submitted;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        for (var i = 1; i <= 500; i++)
            _model.Lines.Add(new InvoiceLine { Description = $"Item {i:000}", Quantity = 1 + i % 9, UnitPrice = 1 });

        var schema = BormBuilder.For<Invoice>()
            .Title("Stock")
            .Array(x => x.Lines, line => line
                    .Field(l => l.Description, f => f.Label("Product").Required())
                    .Field(l => l.Quantity, f => f.Range(0, 9999)),
                f => f.PageSize(20).Attr("itemNoun", "line"))
            .Computed(x => x.Total, m => m.Lines.Sum(l => (decimal)l.Quantity), dependsOn: [nameof(Invoice.Lines)],
                f => f.Label("Items in stock"))   // the model is an invoice; this list is a stock
            .Build();

        _state = new BormState(schema, new BormModelDataAccessor(_model));
    }

    private void HandleSubmit(BormState state) => _submitted = true;

    public void Dispose() => _state.Dispose();
}
